  <section id="how" aria-labelledby="how-h">
    <h2 id="how-h">How the Water Calculator Works</h2>
    <p>It averages two common approaches: the National Academies&rsquo; Adequate Intake (about 80% of which normally comes from drinks) and about 33&nbsp;ml per kg of body weight. It then adds about 0.6&nbsp;L for each hour of exercise and 0.5&nbsp;L for hot weather. These are averages; your thirst and urine color are good day-to-day guides.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="signs" aria-labelledby="signs-h">
    <h2 id="signs-h">Signs You Need More Water</h2>
    <p>Dark yellow urine, thirst, headache, fatigue and dizziness can mean mild dehydration. Older adults feel thirst less, so they benefit from drinking at regular times. Drinking far more than you need in a short time can be dangerous (hyponatremia), especially during long endurance events, so drink to thirst rather than forcing large volumes.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="tips" aria-labelledby="tips-h">
    <h2 id="tips-h">Tips to Drink More Water</h2>
    <p>Keep a bottle in sight, drink a glass with every meal, and add water-rich foods such as fruit, vegetables and soup. Tea, coffee and milk count toward your total. When training, weigh yourself before and after: each pound lost is about 16&ndash;24&nbsp;oz to replace. Pair good hydration with enough <a href="/fiber-calculator/">fiber</a> for healthy digestion.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-h">
    <h2 id="faq-h">Water Intake FAQ</h2>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>How much water should I drink a day?</h3></summary><p>Most women need about 8–11 cups of fluid from drinks and most men 11–15 cups, more with exercise or heat. The calculator gives a personal target from your weight.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Is 8 glasses of water a day right?</h3></summary><p>It is a reasonable minimum for many people, but needs vary with size, activity and climate. Food and other drinks also count.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Does coffee count toward water intake?</h3></summary><p>Yes. Moderate coffee and tea are hydrating overall despite their mild diuretic effect.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>How much water should I drink to lose weight?</h3></summary><p>Drinking water before meals may help you eat less, but there is no special amount for weight loss. Meet your normal needs and replace sugary drinks with water.</p></details>
      <details class="tp-faq"><summary><h3>Can you drink too much water?</h3></summary><p>Yes. Drinking several liters very quickly, or far more than you sweat during endurance exercise, can dilute blood sodium (hyponatremia), which can be dangerous.</p></details>
  </section>

